# Cargo build output, at any depth rather than only at this directory.
#
# Unanchored deliberately. The anchored `/target` this replaces matched only
# `rust-harness/target`, and a cargo run with a *relative* CARGO_TARGET_DIR of
# `rust-harness/target` while the shell was already inside `rust-harness/`
# built into `rust-harness/rust-harness/target` instead -- one directory
# outside the rule, and 3.7 GB of build output was committed before anyone
# noticed. An unanchored pattern cannot be escaped by nesting.
target/

# Harness runtime state, when the binary is run from this directory rather
# than from the container's working directory.
#
# `main.rs` opens `<root>/session.jsonl` on every run, and the interp seam
# writes `probe-flags.jsonl` beside it. In the container that root is
# `/sessions`, which the repository .gitignore already covers; a `cargo run`
# or a profiling run started here puts them in the worktree instead, where
# they show up as untracked files that look like something worth committing
# and are not. They are per-run state: a transcript of one conversation.
#
# Not a test artifact. The tests write into `tempfile::tempdir()` and clean up
# after themselves; this is only ever the real binary having been run.
/session.jsonl
/probe-flags.jsonl

# Profiling and debugging outputs, all written into this directory by the
# targets in the Makefile: perf and cargo-flamegraph (`make flame`) leave
# perf.data, perf.data.old and flamegraph.svg; gdb under `make debug-replay`
# leaves its command history. `make clean` removes them.
/perf.data
/perf.data.old
/flamegraph.svg
/.gdb_history
